ghost of tsushima's director's cut is  out now bringing a proper next  generation version of sucker punches  open world samurai adventure to the  playstation 5. this is pretty  fascinating as the game has already  received a ps5 patch last year which let  it tap into the extra horsepower of the  system so you might be wondering what a  proper ps5 version brings to the table  while it's still largely the same game  there's a number of improvements that  makes ghost of tsushima's director's cut  the definitive version i'm going to be  focusing this video on the ps5 specific  improvements not the iki island  expansion which is also available on the  ps4 if you want to know more about that  check out phil hornshaw's review  let's start with the resolution the  original game on ps4 pro offered a  resolution mode where it ran at a  targeted 30 fps at a checkerboarded  1800p  as well as a performance mode where it  ran at a locked 30 fps at 1080p pretty  straightforward stuff with a simple  choice between picking a more stable  frame rate or a cleaner image  as i mentioned before sucker punch added  a ps5 optimized patch which allowed the  game to take better advantage of that  system the game would then run at 60fps  at 1800p checkerboarded while in  resolution mode essentially making the  performance mode obsolete but the game  was still the ps4 version meaning it  couldn't tap into the full potential of  the system the way a native ps5 version  can so what does the director's cut give  you according to sony if you select  higher resolution mode the game will run  at a dynamic 4k at 60fps digital foundry  has confirmed the game does indeed run  at a checkerboarded 3840 by 2160 so it's  not native 4k but it is really damn  sharp and a noticeable improvement from  the ps4 pro version playing it this way  also takes advantage of the higher  resolution textures sucker punch was  able to add to this version and of  course it runs at a nearly flawless  60fps the result is one of the most  beautiful games you can play on your  playstation 5 right now  but the game still does have a better  frame rate option as well picking this  puts the game to how the original  version played with the ps5 patch 1800p  running at 60fps but why even pick this  mode if the higher resolution also runs  at 60fps according to the devs they  actually considered leaving this option  out but because there is the potential  for a few drop-in frames to occur in  higher resolution they figured they'll  leave in better frame rate for those  that want the option of a locked frame  rate i will say that i never experienced  any frame rate drops during my time  playing on higher resolution and if they  do happen they're so minor that you  won't care so you should 100 play this  game on the higher resolution mode  now let's talk about the power of the  ps5 ssd loading times are fast and i  mean really really fast watch me travel  across the island here and load in  almost instantly  this is on par with what we saw in  spider-man miles morales which is my  benchmark for fast loading times this  increased speed also allows the devs to  do something rather interesting in the  original game a number of cutscenes were  pre-rendered as a way to hide loading  times but now every cutscene is rendered  in real time this was done to help bring  down overall file size as pre-rendered  video files are quite large but it also  allows for one of director's cuts most  welcome features proper japanese lip  sync a point of contention for some  players during the release of ghost of  tsushima was the lack of lip sync while  playing with japanese audio  considering the game's setting and how  playing it in japanese is featured as a  selling point when you start the game  this exclusion did hurt those looking to  experience the game this way because the  sizes of the pre-rendered cutscenes were  so big they could barely fit on the game  disc and didn't leave the space for  alternate cut scenes with different lip  sync now that the cutscenes are rendered  in real time this allows room for sucker  punch to add in japanese lip sync  musk  as somebody who played ghost of tsushima  in japanese this is by far my favorite  new addition it's still not perfect in a  few spots but it's a massive improvement  playing in japanese you don't find  yourself distracted by the lip sync the  way you might have been previously in  fact we have a video comparing the two  versions which you can check out after  this however there is an interesting  quirk i noticed that is a direct result  of switching over to real-time cutscenes  in the cutscene at the beginning of the  game where jyn recovers his sword there  is a flashback to him as a young boy  training notice the excellent match cut  between older and younger gin  however on the ps5 version the game  instead cuts to black and then fades  into the flashback the framing of gin is  also slightly different curious about  this change i reached out to sucker  punch and was told that this is indeed a  direct result of switching over to  real-time cutscenes  the reason the original version was able  to do that seamless match cut is because  it was pre-rendered while switching to  real time made cutting from these two  completely different scenes possible  thanks to the ps5's fast loading the  developers still needed to add a small  transition to make it work the  cinematics team had to go through the  game and tweak a few scenes as needed  while i prefer the look of that original  match cut i do think that sucker punch  made the right call here as the switch  to real-time cutscenes gave us japanese  lip sync something that i find more  important if anything this is just one  of those interesting examples of the  kind of changes made to a game when  adapting it to new hardware the  director's cut also gets expected ps5  features such as dual sense and 3d audio  the haptics ride that sweet spot of  feeling good without being too much  there's the obvious stuff like the  collection of swords or tension of a bow  but there's also more subtle effects  like your horse running on a rocky path  or feeling the wind blow past you  i hesitate to go so far as calling it  game changing but i do think it adds a  lot to the experience you even get some  sounds coming out of the controller  during cut scenes such as when jin  unsheaths his sword  other notable changes include the much  asked for lock-on feature which you can  turn on and off as well as some  alternate button styles great for  accessibility however these options are  included in a free update that ps4  players will get to enjoy as well  which brings up the question of if this  is worth picking up if you've already  played gosu fushima originally the  pricing of the ps5 upgrade is a bit  confusing to wrap your head around  buying the ps4 version of the director's  cut doesn't come with any of the  improvements we just mentioned just the  icky island expansion and for that it  will cost you 20 us dollars meanwhile  the ps5 upgrade runs to 30 dollars so  you're paying an extra 10 dollars for  all of these improvements and while i do  think these improvements do make the  game better and it's worth checking out  if you haven't already played ghost  tsushima if you've already played it  before it's a bit of a tough sell  however i leave it up to you to decide  if it's worth it a big selling point  might be the iki island expansion which  if you want to know more about i'll  remind you again to go check out phil  hornshaw's review spoiler alert you  liked it and if you liked this video why  not give it a thumbs up and sub to  gamespot for more coverage on all things  video games thanks for watching  [Music]  you
 

